{{was wotd|2009|June|26}}
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|urbanité}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|urbanitas}}, from {{m|la|urbanus||belonging to a city}}, with a sense of "having the manners of townspeople" in [[w:Classical Latin|Classical Latin]], from {{m|la|urbs||city}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/əːˈbæn.ɪ.ti/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ɝˈbæn.ə.ti/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|urbanities}}

# [[behaviour|Behaviour]] that is [[polished]], [[refined]], [[courteous]].
#* '''1825''', [[w:Washington Irving|Washington Irving]], "[[s:The Crayon Papers/Paris at the Restoration|Paris at the Restoration]]", ''The Crayon Papers'',
#*: The vaunted courtesy of the old school, the smooth '''urbanity''' that prevailed in former days {{...}}
#* '''1864''', [[w:Sheridan Le Fanu|Sheridan Le Fanu]], ''Wylder's Hand'', [[s:Wylder's Hand/Chapter XLVII|Chapter XLVII]],
#*: Wealdon's two little visits explained perfectly the active '''urbanities''' of Captain Stanley Lake.
#* {{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=9|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Eustace gaped at him in amazement. When his '''urbanity''' dropped away from him, as now, he had an innocence of expression which was almost infantile. It was as if the world had never touched him at all.}}
# What is [[characteristically]] [[urban]] in an area; [[urbanness]].
#* '''1955''', C.J. Lammers, ''Studies in Holland flood disaster 1953'', vol. 2, [http://books.google.ca/books?id=_DcrAAAAYAAJ&vq=urbanity&pg=PA39 p. 39],
#*: {{...}}, the majority of cases will differ as to "'''urbanity'''", as most of the evacuees were rural.
#* '''1956''', Fred C. Iklé & Harry V. Kincaid, "Social Aspects of Wartime Evacuation of American Cities", ''Disaster Study'', vol 4., [http://books.google.ca/books?id=yTIrAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA2-PA44 p. 44],
#*: Evacuees, the majority of whom were rural persons, reported more tensions as the '''urbanity''' of the reception community increased

